@startuml automaton-implementations
' The three Levenshtein-automaton implementations the crate offers, and when to
' use each. All share the position/subsumption model; they differ in WHEN the
' automaton is built and HOW the edit operations are fixed.
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName "DejaVu Sans"
skinparam rectangle {
  RoundCorner 14
  BorderColor #1565C0
  FontColor #102027
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowThickness 2

title Three automaton implementations — same position model, different trade-offs

rectangle "Shared model\nposition <latex>\langle i,e\rangle</latex> · subsumption ≤χ · characteristic vector χ" as M #BBDEFB

rectangle "Lazy / parameterized   (default)\nsrc/transducer/{query,state,transition,pool}\n• states materialised on demand during the query\n• one W-independent transition rule driven by χ\n• best general default — no precompute" as L #E3F2FD
rectangle "Universal / eager\nsrc/transducer/universal/\n• parameter-free DFA precomputed once per k\n• reused across every query word\n• best when k is fixed and queries are many" as U #E3F2FD
rectangle "Generalized / runtime\nsrc/transducer/generalized/\n• OperationSet chosen at run time\n• drives weighted f64 & phonetic edits\n• +10–20% overhead for full flexibility" as G #C5CAE9

M -down-> L
M -down-> U
M -down-> G

note bottom of L
  <latex>\text{Algorithm} \in \{ \text{Standard}, \text{Transposition}, \text{MergeAndSplit} \}</latex>
  selected as a zero-cost marker type.
end note
note bottom of G
  Indigo tint = also backs the real-valued
  (OperationCostsF64) and articulatory cost paths.
end note
@enduml
